Falsifies `system_tests::user::spawn_mapping_revocation_tests::a_page_the_kernel_wired_is_unmapped_when_its_frame_is_revoked`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the tree as it stood until 2026-09-21: `AddressSpace::map_physical` maps a page and
files no record, so the mapping is in no space's mapping log, and every unmap sweep in
`crate::revoke` is driven by that log. A `Spawn::maps` entry, a `DeviceRun`, the initrd and the
`x86_64` timebase page were therefore invisible to `PageFrame::REVOKE`, `DeviceFrame::REVOKE` and
`MemoryRegion::DESTROY` alike: the capability went and the mapping stayed.

**The patch is larger than the fix, and every extra hunk exists to keep the tree honest rather than
to widen the defect.** The fix moved the record from `user_address_space_map` into `map_physical`,
so simply deleting it from `map_physical` would take the `MAP_INTO` path's record with it, which is
a second and different defect (`user::live_swap_tests` catches that one within a minute, on its
`LOG_REVOKE_ENFORCED` assertion, which is worth knowing). So this patch puts that record back where
it was, and restores the explicit `record_mapping` calls that `crate::revoke`'s own suite used to
carry beside each `map_physical`. What is left is exactly one difference from the fixed tree: the
kernel-wiring callers record nothing.

It was found by asking the question risk 7's adversarial pass left open, which recorded this in
`map_physical`'s own `BUGS` as latent. It is not latent, and the reachable path is in the test
module's header: `fs_service::spawn_fs_server` wires the file channel's shared pages into the FS
server through `Spawn::maps`, and `boot_progenitor` hands the progenitor `PageFrame(file_shared, 1)`
with `GRANT` over the first of them.

Expected to fail: `assert!(mmu::translate_at(wired_root, WIRED_VA).is_none(), "a page the kernel
wired into an address space survived the revoke of its frame ...")`. Confirmed red on 2026-09-21, on
aarch64, before the fix existed, and it is the only test in the suite that fails under this patch.

**The two assertions above the headline stay green under this patch**, which is what makes the red
mean the claim rather than the fixture. The vacuity guard says the frame really was mapped in both
spaces, so there was something for the revoke to miss; the premise check says the revoke really did
reach the mapping a sweep can see. A revoke that did nothing at all would fail the second one with a
message saying the test proved nothing.

**Read the architecture line the way the audit in milestone 313 (the security audit that was due
since August: userspace confinement, read adversarially) taught.** `script/falsifications` replays a
kernel record on the one architecture its patch names, and nothing here is architecture-specific:
the mapping log, the sweeps and `map_physical` are portable kernel code. The record is aarch64's
because that is where it was replayed, not because the defect is; the test passes on aarch64,
riscv64 and x86_64.

**Re-cut 2026-10-04 (UTC) by the map-revocation-window lane**, when the record moved into
`revoke::MappingHold` and `MAP_INTO` stopped going through `user_address_space_map`. The extra
hunks now call `hold().record_mapping` where they called the free `record_mapping`, and `MAP_INTO`
needs no hunk: it records through `map_physical_held`, which this patch leaves alone. The one
difference from the fixed tree is unchanged. Replayed red on aarch64 the same day at
`spawn_mapping_revocation_tests.rs:145`, the headline.

The `MAP_INTO` engine's context once carried a comment citing milestone 19 (run a real workload),
whose `d` phase is where the I-cache sync beside it came from; the gloss stays because a patch's
context lines are the tree's text and the citation gate reads them.

**Re-cut 2026-10-11 (UTC) by milestone 872 (the kernel's user-mode file is split along its seams)**,
when `impl AddressSpace` moved to `kernel/src/user/address_space.rs`. Regenerated from a tree with
this patch applied, so every hunk now sits at its real line; the registry hunk had applied only
by offset since milestone 812 (`std::thread::spawn` runs real threads in one address space) moved
it. The one difference from the fixed tree is unchanged.

